    "# A list is like a shopping list where we can add items, remove items, or change items whenever we want. It's mutable (changeable/editable). They are created with square brackets [].\n",
    "# A tuple is like a list of names carved into a stone tablet. Once we create it, we cannot change, add, or remove anything. It's immutable (unchangeable/uneditable). Tuples are created with parentheses ().\n",
    "\n",
    "# Examples\n",
    "\n",
    "# A list (mutable)\n",
    "my_list = [1, 'apple', 3.14]\n",
    "my_list[1] = 'orange'  # This works perfectly!\n",
    "print(my_list)\n",
    "\n",
    "# A tuple (immutable)\n",
    "my_tuple = (1, 'apple', 3.14)\n",
    "# my_tuple[1] = 'orange'  # This would cause an error!\n",
    "print(my_tuple)"
   ]
