    "# What are Python's data types? Give examples.\n",
    "\n",
    "# In Python, data types represent the kind of value that a variable holds, which determines what operations you can perform on it. Because Python is dynamically typed, we don't need to explicitly declare a variable's type. That is, Python automatically figures it out based on the value you assign to it!\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "# Examples\n",
    "\n",
    "# Integer (int): Whole numbers. x = 10\n",
    "# Float (float): Numbers with a decimal point. y = 3.14\n",
    "# String (str): Text, wrapped in quotes. name = \"Alice\"\n",
    "# Boolean (bool): Represents truth, can only be True or False. is_active = True\n",
    "# List (list): A changeable, ordered collection of items. my_list = [1, 2, 3]\n",
    "# Tuple (tuple): An unchangeable, ordered collection of items. my_tuple = (1, 2, 3)\n",
    "# Dictionary (dict): A collection of key-value pairs. my_dict = {\"name\": \"Bob\", \"age\": 30}"
   ]
